# Line endings — the rule is LF, everywhere, working tree included.
#
# ⚠️ git does NOT "want CRLF". Every blob git stores has always been LF here, and
# still is. The CRLF came from ONE thing: a machine-GLOBAL `core.autocrlf=true`,
# which converts to CRLF on checkout and back to LF on commit. That setting is not
# this repo's, it is not the same on every clone, and it is what produced the
# `warning: LF will be replaced by CRLF the next time Git touches it` noise —
# emitted whenever a tool wrote a file with LF, which is every tool used here.
#
# So the conversion itself was the problem, not the direction of it. Turning it OFF
# for this tree makes the warning impossible rather than merely quiet: with no
# conversion there is nothing to warn about. `.gitattributes` is the right place
# because it travels with the repo and OVERRIDES each developer's `core.autocrlf`,
# so the answer does not depend on who cloned it.
#
# 🔒 Why LF and not CRLF in the working tree:
#   - the committed bytes are LF regardless, so LF in the tree means the file on
#     disk and the file in git are byte-identical — diffs, hashes and patches all
#     line up, and `git status` cannot be dirtied by line endings alone;
#   - everything this project builds with reads LF on Windows without complaint —
#     Zig, Deno, Bun, `zig cc`, and the `.wat`/`.wast` corpora;
#   - a `.wat` fixture's bytes are INPUT to an assembler and, through `pin`, to a
#     SHA-256 digest. A line ending that depends on the checkout would change a
#     digest, and this project compares digests against a sibling runtime.
#
# ⚠️ If a binary file is ever added, mark it `binary` here BEFORE committing it.
# Nothing tracked today is binary, which is the only reason `*` can be this blunt.

* text=auto eol=lf

# Explicit, so a new contributor does not have to infer the rule from `*`.
*.zig   text eol=lf
*.zon   text eol=lf
*.c     text eol=lf
*.h     text eol=lf
*.rs    text eol=lf
*.mjs   text eol=lf
*.json  text eol=lf
*.md    text eol=lf
*.txt   text eol=lf
*.wat   text eol=lf
*.wast  text eol=lf

# Not tracked today — listed so the first one added is handled, not discovered.
*.wasm  binary
*.exe   binary
*.lib   binary
*.dll   binary
*.png   binary
*.key   binary
